.TH RSH 1 "November 2026" "rsh 1.0" "WolOS"
.SH NAME
rsh \- Ruby with command lines: a script interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rsh
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file " [" arguments ]]
.br
.B rsh \-c
.I text
.br
.B rsh \-s
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rsh
runs Ruby. A statement whose first word is not something Ruby knows is a
.I command line
instead, written the way you write it in a terminal: pipes, redirects,
.BR && ,
.BR || ,
file name patterns, here-documents and
.BR & .
So one file can hold both, and it can start with
.B #!/usr/bin/rsh
and be run as a program.
.PP
With a file name,
.B rsh
runs that file; the arguments after it are
.BR ARGV .
With
.B \-c
or
.B \-e
it runs the text. With
.B \-s
it reads the script from standard input (this is how
.B "wol script on"
sends a script to another computer). With nothing, on a terminal, it is
interactive.
.SS Ruby files
Every file has the shell layer, whatever its name: a
.B .rb
file is read like a
.B .rsh
file. A file whose first line (or the line after a
.B #!
line) is the comment
.B "# rsh: ruby"
is plain Ruby: no line is a command line, and the file means what it means in Ruby.
.SS Metaprogramming
Bare words are classified when the file is read, so a method made at run time (by
.B method_missing
or by
.B define_method
over a list) is a command unless the file declares it:
.B "rsh_methods :name, ..."
names such methods,
.B "ruby do ... end"
(or
.BR "ruby { ... }" )
makes every bare word inside Ruby, and
.B ruby!
alone on a line does that to the end of the file. Literal names after
.BR define_method ,
.BR attr_reader ,
.BR alias_method ,
.B Struct.new
and
.B Data.define
are known without a declaration.
.B eval
is plain Ruby;
.BI Rsh.eval( text " [, " binding ])
reads the text with the shell layer.
.SS How a line is read
Only the start of a statement is looked at: the start of the file, after a new line or
.BR ; ,
after
.BR then ,
.BR do ,
.BR else ,
after
.B {
and after the words
.BR if ,
.BR unless ,
.BR while ,
.B until
and
.BR elsif .
There the line is a command line when its first word is a plain word, is not a Ruby keyword, is not a
variable you set earlier, is not a method defined in the file (or in a file loaded with
.BR require_relative ),
is not one of Ruby's own words (puts, print, p, printf, format, require, raise, loop, exit, rand,
gets, warn, lambda, proc and a few more; "sleep" is a command), and is not followed straight away by
.BR .\ ,
.BR (\  ,
.BR [\  ,
.B =
and the like. A word that starts with
.BR ./ ,
.BR ../ ,
.B /
or
.B ~
is always a command.
.PP
To be sure, put
.B "$ "
in front to force a command line (so
.B "$ printf"
runs the program, not Ruby's printf),
or write a call with brackets, like
.BR foo(1) ,
to force Ruby.
.B rsh \-\-explain
.I file
shows how each line is read: R for Ruby, C for a command line, X for a back-quote capture inside Ruby.
.SS Command lines
Words, single quotes and double quotes work as in a shell. Anywhere in a word, quoted or not,
.BI #{ expression }
puts a Ruby value in. A value is always exactly one argument, whatever it holds (spaces, quotes,
semicolons, a leading dash): nothing is split again and no text is ever handed to
.BR /bin/sh .
An Array gives one argument for each element.
.B $NAME
and
.B ${NAME}
read the environment.
.TP
.B | and |&
Pipe the output (and with
.B |&
the error output) into the next stage. A stage can be a Ruby block:
.B "seq 1 9 | { |line| (line.to_i * 2).to_s } | cat"
runs the block for each line. A String replaces the line, nil or false drops it, any other value keeps it.
The status of a pipeline is the last one that failed.
.TP
.B "> >> < 2> 2>> 2>&1 &> <<<"
Redirects, and
.B >|
(the same as
.BR > ;
there is no noclobber). A file name written as
.B #{path}
is one word. Another descriptor is one digit (\fB3>file\fR); a number of two digits or
.B {name}>file
is a mistake that says to quote it.
.TP
.B "<<WORD  <<~WORD  <<'WORD'"
Here-documents. The first and second take
.B #{}
values; the third is taken literally.
.TP
.B "* ? [a-z] {a,b} **"
File name patterns, only in unquoted text. A pattern that matches nothing is an error;
.B "Rsh.nullglob = true"
turns it into no words.
.B ~
and
.B ~name
are home directories.
.TP
.B "&& || ;"
Run the next command if the first worked, if it failed, or in any case.
.TP
.B &
Run in the background. See
.B jobs
below.
.TP
.B NAME=value command
Sets a variable for that one command.
.PP
A back-quoted text (or
.BR %x{} )
runs a command line and gives its output as a String; its status is in
.BR $? ;
one whose command fails raises
.B Rsh::Failed
like any failed command (outside a condition, unless
.BR relaxed! ).
After
.B &&
or
.B ||
the next word is read as at the start of a statement: a local, a method of the file, a Ruby word
such as
.BR puts ,
or a Capitalised name makes the rest Ruby
.RB ( "make && notify \(dqdone\(dq" ,
.BR "cmd || puts \(dqfailed\(dq" );
.B "$ cmd"
forces a command.
In a command line,
.B $?
is the status of the part that ran last
.RB ( "cmd || echo \(dqfailed: $?\(dq" ),
while the
.B #{...}
values of a line are all read once, when the line starts.
A command line is also a value:
.B true
or
.B false
by its status, so
.B "if grep \-q root /etc/passwd"
and
.B "ok = (make test)"
work.
.SS Failure
A command line that fails and is not used as a condition (not after
.BR if ,
.BR unless ,
.B while
or
.BR until ,
not left of
.B &&
or
.BR || )
raises
.B Rsh::Failed
with
.BR status ,
.B argv
and
.B stderr
(the end of what it wrote on the error output). Left alone it ends the script with a message such as
.B "rsh: deploy.rsh:12: make exited 2"
and the same exit status. Use
.B begin ... rescue Rsh::Failed => e
to handle it, an
.B ||
to ignore it, or
.B relaxed!
to carry on after every failure (then look at
.BR $? ).
.B $?
is an
.B Rsh::Status
with
.BR exitstatus ,
.BR success? ,
.BR signaled? ,
.B termsig
and
.B statuses
(one for each pipeline stage). In the interactive shell nothing is raised.
.SS Commands built in
.B cd
(also
.B "cd dir do ... end"
to go back afterwards),
.BR pushd ,
.BR popd ,
.BR dirs ,
.BR export ,
.BR unset ,
.BR exec ,
.BR umask ,
.BR jobs ,
.BR fg ,
.BR bg ,
.BR wait ,
.BR source ,
.BR echo ,
.BR type ,
.B pwd
and
.BR history .
.B "export NAME=value"
sets the environment;
.B "export NAME"
keeps a NAME that is set and is a mistake for one that is not
.RB ( FOO=1
is Ruby in rsh, not the environment: write
.BR "export FOO=#{FOO}" ).
.B "source file words"
runs the file in this process with ARGV set to the words while it runs.
.SS The Ruby inside
Ruby with the usual core classes, plus
.BR JSON ,
.BR Regexp ,
.BR Set ,
.BR Time ,
.BR File ,
.BR Dir ,
.BR ENV ,
.B Process
and
.BR Etc ,
with
.B eval
and
.BR binding .
For scripts rsh adds
.BR File.stat ,
.BR File.lstat ,
.BR File.ftype ,
.BR File.readable? ,
.BR File.writable? ,
.BR File.executable? ,
.BR File.owned? ,
.BR File.mtime ,
.BR Process.uid ,
.BR Process.euid ,
.BR Process.gid ,
.BR Process.egid ,
.BR Dir.home ,
and, after
.BR "require \(dqtime\(dq" ,
.B Time#iso8601
and
.B Time.parse
for ISO 8601 times.
.B gets
reads standard input, never the files named in ARGV. There is no network library: use a command
(curl, wol) for that.
.B system
takes separate arguments only. No text ever goes to
.BR /bin/sh :
.B open("|cmd")
and
.B IO.popen
are errors. A NUL byte in an argument, a variable value or a file name is an error.
.B umask
takes octal digits only.
.SS Sh: the shell things as Ruby values
.B Sh
is built in (no
.BR require ;
it loads when first used). Its helpers work on the files themselves, start no program and give Ruby
values: an Array of paths to hand to a command (one argument each), a String, true or false.
.BR "Sh.find(dir, name:, type:, older:, newer:, maxdepth:, hidden:)" ,
.BR "Sh.grep(pattern, *files)" ,
.BR Sh.which ,
.BR Sh.exists? ,
.BR file? ,
.BR dir? ,
.BR age ,
.BR size ,
.BR du ,
.BR read ,
.BR write ,
.BR append ,
.BR lines ,
.BR mkdir_p ,
.BR touch ,
.BR cp ,
.BR cp_r ,
.BR mv ,
.BR ln_s ,
.BR chmod ,
.BR rm ,
.B rm_rf
and
.B "Sh.tmpdir { |dir| ... }"
(removed afterwards). The Ruby-standard
.B FileUtils
(no options),
.BR Find.find ,
.B Shellwords
and
.B Dir.mktmpdir
are built in too. A command line keeps its meaning:
.B "find . \-name x"
is the program.
.PP
.nf
# pack the logs older than a week, then say what is left
old = Sh.find("logs", name: "*.log", older: 7 * 86400)
if old.any?
  tar \-czf old\-logs.tgz #{old}
  Sh.rm(old)
end
puts "packed #{old.size}, kept #{Sh.find("logs", type: :file).size}"
Sh.tmpdir do |dir|
  Sh.cp_r("logs", dir)
  puts Sh.grep("ok", Sh.find(dir, name: "*.log"))
end
abort "rsync is missing" unless Sh.which("rsync")
.fi
.PP
A pattern with no match and a redirect that cannot open its file are a failed command with status 1:
false in a condition
.RB ( "ls *.log || echo none" ),
.B Rsh::Failed
outside one.
.B Rsh.last_pid
is the pid of the last
.BR "cmd &" ;
.B "wait PID"
waits for it. A
.B source
whose path is written out is read ahead, so its methods are Ruby at a bare call.
.SS Interactive use
On a terminal
.B rsh
is a shell: a line editor with history (kept in
.IR ~/.local/state/rsh/history ,
mode 0600; a line that starts with a space or looks like it holds a secret, such as
.BR "export API_TOKEN=..." ,
is not kept), Ctrl-R search, Tab completion of commands, files, your variables and
methods and environment variables (\fB$HO\fR), Ctrl-T, Ctrl-Y, Ctrl-V and Alt-D, lines that go on while the Ruby is not finished, job control (Ctrl-Z,
.BR fg ,
.BR bg ),
and colours while you type: the command word is green when the program exists and red when it does
not. If
.I ~/.config/rsh/init.rsh
exists it runs first; a method named
.B prompt
in it gives the prompt text. The start-up file and the history are used only when they are plain
files you own that others cannot write; as root the home comes from
.IR /etc/passwd ,
not from
.BR $HOME .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " text" ", " \-e " text"
Run the text.
.TP
.B \-s
Read the script from standard input. Arguments come after
.BR \-\- .
.TP
.BI \-\-name " file"
With
.BR \-s :
the file name the script has, for messages and
.BR $0 .
.TP
.B \-n
Read and compile the script and run nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-lint
Like
.BR \-n ,
with warnings for command words that are not on PATH, text built into
.BR "sh \-c" ,
world-writable
.BR chmod ,
fixed
.I /tmp
names and a value as the first argument of a program. Exit status 0 when clean, 1 with warnings
only, 2 with errors.
.TP
.B \-\-explain
Show the mode (shell or ruby), then how each line is read.
.TP
.B \-\-ruby
Show the Ruby text that will run.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-l
Interactive.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the options.
.TP
.B \-\-
The end of the options: the next word is the file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The script's own
.BR exit ;
else the status of the failed command that ended it (128+N when a signal N ended it); 130 when
interrupted; 1 for a Ruby error; 2 for a mistake in the script itself found before it runs, or a
bad option; 127 if the file cannot be read or a command is not found; 126 if a command cannot run (a
file that is not executable, a folder given as the script);
70 if the interpreter cannot start. A script that runs to its end is 0, even when its last command
line was a condition that failed. These are frozen (docs/STABILITY.md in the source).
.PP
Every message is one line on standard error that starts with
.BR rsh: ,
then the place as
.BI FILE : LINE :
when there is one; a script read from standard input is the file
.BR \- .
.SH SIGNALS
TERM, HUP and INT end a script by raising SignalException (Interrupt for INT) at its next
safe point (a command line, an iteration of
.BR loop ,
the end of
.BR sleep ),
so
.B ensure
clauses and
.B at_exit
blocks run; rsh then ends by that signal (status 128+N).
.B trap
(or
.BR Signal.trap )
takes a block,
.B IGNORE
or
.BR DEFAULT .
Code that reaches no safe point (a
.B while true
with nothing in it) is ended by the signal itself after 2 seconds, or by a second one that comes a second after the first.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where
.I rsh/init.rsh
is read (interactive only).
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the history is kept.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/rsh/init.rsh
Your start-up file for the interactive shell.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/rsh/history
The history.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#!/usr/bin/rsh
dest = ARGV[0] || "/srv/app"
cd ~/src/app
git pull \-\-ff\-only && make \-j4
rsync \-a build/ #{dest}/
errors = `journalctl \-b \-p err`.lines.reject { |l| l =~ /fwupd/ }
puts "#{errors.size} errors" unless errors.empty?
.fi
.SH COMPATIBILITY
Since 1.0 the language, the options, the exit statuses, the shape of the messages and the names
and parameters of
.B Sh
(and of the
.BR FileUtils ,
.BR Find ,
.B Shellwords
and
.B Dir.mktmpdir
built in) are frozen: a script that works on 1.0 works on every 1.x with the same output and
statuses. Only a 2.0 changes them. Nothing is deprecated. A 1.x may add
.B Sh
helpers and options, methods of the built-in Ruby, options of
.BR rsh ,
a meaning for the syntax that is a mistake today (a descriptor above 9 such as
.BR 10>f ,
.BR {fd}>f ,
.BR <&\- ,
.BR >&\- ,
.BR N<<< ),
clearer message text and new
.B \-\-lint
warnings. The interactive editing is not part of the promise. The full list is
.I docs/COMPAT-1.0.md
in the source.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wol (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR ruby (1)
